  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R3, R5, R9, R17, and R19 establish mechanical alloying/high-energy milling followed by SPS as a route to homogeneous, refined HEA solid solutions and show that milling time controls refinement, hardness, and contamination. R3, R5, R9, and R10 directly support grain-boundary/Hall–Petch strengthening, including in nanocrystalline material, while R7, R9, R12, and R14 support lattice-distortion and solid-solution strengthening. R4 additionally links severe deformation, defect accumulation, refinement, and FCC/BCC-related phase evolution in a closely related Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy family. These teachings would allow an expert to propose HEBM plus SPS for CoCrFeNiTi and expect strengthening from refined grains, accumulated defects, and solid-solution distortion. The precise 1–6 h milling window, 900–1000 °C SPS conditions, 83% FCC/17% BCC balance, and quantitative properties are not specifically disclosed, but they are optimization details rather than a missing central concept."
